An array gives direct access to the node at each position. I store the node references, unlink the selected node, and return the original head unless the first node is removed.

This is easy to check, but it uses extra memory. For `10 -> 20 -> 30` and `k = 2`, the array points to `20`, then the code changes `10.next` to `30`.

```javascript
class Solution {
    deleteKthNode(head, k) {
        if (head === null || k <= 0) {
            return head;
        }

        const nodes = [];
        let current = head;

        while (current !== null) {
            nodes.push(current);
            current = current.next;
        }

        if (k > nodes.length) {
            return head;
        }

        if (k === 1) {
            return head.next;
        }

        nodes[k - 2].next = nodes[k - 1].next;
        return head;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the array of node references.

This version first counts the nodes, then finds the node just before position `k`. Counting first lets the code reject an invalid position before changing the list.

For a list with six nodes and `k = 4`, the count is six, then the pointer stops at node three and links it to node five. The original nodes are reused, so no new list is built.

```java
class Solution {
    public ListNode deleteKthNode(ListNode head, int k) {
        if (head == null || k <= 0) {
            return head;
        }

        int length = 0;
        ListNode current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            length++;
            current = current.next;
        }

        if (k > length) {
            return head;
        }

        if (k == 1) {
            return head.next;
        }

        current = head;
        for (int position = 1; position < k - 1; position++) {
            current = current.next;
        }

        current.next = current.next.next;
        return head;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, with at most two list passes.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

Only the previous node needs to be kept while walking to position `k`. Once it is found, the target is skipped by changing one link. The input list is modified in place.

The empty-list case and a request for the first node are handled separately. A position past the end does not change the head.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public ListNode deleteKthNode(ListNode head, int k) {
        if (head == null || k <= 0) {
            return head;
        }

        if (k == 1) {
            return head.next;
        }

        ListNode previous = head;
        int position = 1;

        while (previous.next != null && position < k - 1) {
            previous = previous.next;
            position++;
        }

        if (position == k - 1 && previous.next != null) {
            previous.next = previous.next.next;
        }

        return head;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

A dummy node makes the first-node case easier. After moving to the node before position `k`, its `next` pointer can skip the node being removed. The list itself is changed, and the new head is returned.

If `k` is zero or larger than the list length, this code leaves the list unchanged.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* deleteKthNode(ListNode* &head, int k) {
        if (head == nullptr || k <= 0) {
            return head;
        }

        ListNode dummy(0, head);
        ListNode* previous = &dummy;

        for (int position = 1; position < k && previous->next != nullptr; position++) {
            previous = previous->next;
        }

        if (previous->next != nullptr) {
            previous->next = previous->next->next;
        }

        head = dummy.next;
        return head;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

## Intuition
to delete the kth node in a singly linked list, we first need to reach the node just before it (the (k-1)th node). Once we're there, we can skip the kth node by changing the next pointer.

The only special case is when k = 1, because deleting the first node changes the head of the linked list. In that case, we simply return head.next.

## Approach
-If the linked list is empty (head == null), return null.
-If k == 1, delete the head node by returning head.next.
-Otherwise, start traversing the list from the head.
Keep a counter to identify when you've reached the (k-1)th node.
Once you reach it, update its next pointer to skip the kth node:

curr.next = curr.next.next;
Return the original head of the modified linked list.

## Solution
```java[]
/*
Definition of singly linked list:
class ListNode{
    public int data;
    public ListNode next;
    ListNode() { data = 0; next = null; }
    ListNode(int x) { data = x; next = null; }
    ListNode(int x, ListNode next) { data = x; this.next = next; }
}
*/

class Solution {
    public ListNode deleteKthNode(ListNode head, int k) {
        // Your code goes here
        if(head == null)
        {
            return null;
        }
      // if k =1, making the 2nd element as the head
        if(k==1){

            return head.next;
        }
        // k>1
        ListNode curr = head;
        int count = 0;
        while(curr != null && curr.next != null)
        {   count ++;
            if(count == k-1)
            {
                curr.next = curr.next.next;
               break;

            }
            curr = curr.next;


        }
        return head;


    }
}
```

## Complexity
Time Complexity:
In the worst case, we traverse the linked list once until the (k-1)th node.
Time Complexity: O(n)

Space Complexity:
We only use: one pointer (curr), one integer (count)
No extra data structures are used.
Space Complexity: O(1)

